# Copy to .env and edit for the environment you are running. This template
# contains only published sandbox credentials and local service defaults; do
# not use those defaults for a production VPS. Real credentials belong in the
# resulting .env, never in this tracked example. Restrict the resulting file to
# its owner (chmod 600 .env) and keep it out of Git.
#
# Every credential below can instead be given as a file: KEY_FILE names a file
# whose contents are KEY — e.g. DATABASE_URL_FILE=/run/secrets/database_url.
# That keeps it out of the process environment, for a deployment that mounts
# its credentials as Compose or Kubernetes secrets. Setting both KEY and
# KEY_FILE is refused. See docs/configuration.md.
#
# To deploy, start from deploy/standard or deploy/tunnel instead: each has an
# .env.example holding only what that deployment needs. See docs/deploy/.

# --- Server ---
PORT=8080
# Public origin of the site, used to build absolute URLs. No trailing slash.
BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080
LOG_LEVEL=info
# json, or gcp to rename level/msg to severity/message for Google Cloud Logging.
# Without it every line files under DEFAULT severity there, so severity filters
# and alerts match nothing.
LOG_FORMAT=json
SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=15

# --- Database ---
# For running the binary on the host (`make run`). The compose server ignores
# this and talks to its own postgres service, so copying this file cannot
# accidentally point the container at the wrong database. Port 5433 is the
# compose stack's host port.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://goevent:goevent@localhost:5433/goevent?sslmode=disable
# Used only by the test suite. Tests are skipped when it is unset.
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://goevent:goevent@localhost:5433/goevent?sslmode=disable

# --- Site ---
# The organisation's name, shown in the header, page titles and emails.
SITE_NAME=goevent
CURRENCY=ZAR

# --- Admin ---
# Nothing here is required, and there is no admin credential in this file at all.
# Administrator accounts live in the database: on its first start against an empty
# admin_users the server prints a one-time setup token, and /admin/setup exchanges
# it for the first account. Managing accounts after that is the admin's own job,
# at /admin/users.
#
# Optional, for a deploy that cannot read a token out of a log line: supply the
# token instead of having one generated. 32 characters minimum — while it is
# unclaimed it is the credential for the whole admin area.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# SETUP_TOKEN=
# Optional. How long a sign-in lasts; defaults to 24. A session is a row in
# admin_sessions, so shortening this is not the way to revoke one — disabling the
# account or changing its password ends every session it has, immediately.
# SESSION_TTL_HOURS=24

# BASE_URL matters beyond absolute links: it decides whether cookies are marked
# Secure, and the scheme the CSRF origin check compares against. Getting it wrong
# on an HTTPS deployment refuses every form with a 403.

# --- Payments ---
# Both are optional. With neither, the site runs free events and registrations
# an administrator marks paid by hand (cash or EFT). With both, the registration
# form asks which to use; with one, it does not ask at all.

# --- PayFast ---
# Leave PAYFAST_MERCHANT_ID unset to run without PayFast. Otherwise the merchant
# id and key are both required. The values below are PayFast's own published sandbox
# credentials, which appear in their documentation — they are not secret, and
# they take no real money. Replace all three with your own before going live.
#   Sandbox dashboard: https://sandbox.payfast.co.za
PAYFAST_MERCHANT_ID=10000100
PAYFAST_MERCHANT_KEY=46f0cd694581a
# The account's "salt passphrase". It must match the PayFast dashboard exactly:
# set on one side only and every signature fails. jt7NOE43FZPn is the sandbox's.
PAYFAST_PASSPHRASE=jt7NOE43FZPn
# Defaults to true. Set it to false only with live credentials in place — it is
# the difference between a test transaction and somebody's actual card.
PAYFAST_SANDBOX=true
#
# Return, cancel and notify URLs are derived from BASE_URL, so there is nothing
# to keep in step. The exception is the notify URL, which PayFast's *servers*
# must reach: localhost is not reachable from the internet, so testing a real
# payment locally needs a tunnel, and this is where its hostname goes.
#   e.g. PAYFAST_NOTIFY_URL=https://abc123.ngrok.app/payments/payfast/callback
# PAYFAST_NOTIFY_URL=
#
# Optional. Comma-separated CIDRs a notification may come from, overriding
# PayFast's published ranges — they have changed before, and adding one should
# not need a new release. The literal value "any" disables the check: it is for
# testing against the sandbox, which does not necessarily notify from the
# production ranges, and it is never right in production.
# PAYFAST_ALLOWED_CIDRS=any

# --- SnapScan ---
# South Africa's QR payment app. Leave SNAPSCAN_SNAP_CODE unset to run without
# it; set it and the other two become required.
#
# ⚠ THERE IS NO SANDBOX. Unlike PayFast, whose safe default above is a test
# account, any SnapScan configuration here takes real money. There is no test
# snap code to develop against: the smallest real payment you are willing to make
# is the test.
#
# All three come from SnapScan merchant support.
#   Docs: https://developer.snapscan.co.za
# SNAPSCAN_SNAP_CODE=shopalot
# SNAPSCAN_API_KEY=
#
# The shared secret a notification's HMAC is computed with. Required, because the
# callback route is the only thing that can mark a payment paid and this is what
# proves a notification came from SnapScan.
# SNAPSCAN_WEBHOOK_AUTH_KEY=
#
# Optional: the Secure QR Payload validation key, which SnapScan enables per
# account on request. With it, the amount and payment reference in the payment
# URL are signed and cannot be edited between this page and the scan. Without
# it, strict mode still fixes a minimum amount and refuses a repeat payment on
# the same reference.
# SNAPSCAN_VALIDATION_KEY=
#
# There is no notify URL here: SnapScan support configures the webhook address on
# the merchant account. It must point at BASE_URL + /payments/snapscan/callback,
# which for local testing means a tunnel's hostname.

# --- Secret key ---
# REQUIRED. The server's one secret: it encrypts pending email and signs
# registrants' manage links and ticket codes. This public key is for development
# only. In production generate with `openssl rand -hex 32`, and keep it: changing
# it invalidates every link and ticket already sent. Back it up with the
# database. SECRET_KEY_FILE is supported.
SECRET_KEY=abababababababababababababababababababababababababababababababab

# --- Email ---
# REQUIRED. SMTP_HOST and EMAIL_FROM must both be set, or the Microsoft Graph
# settings below, or the server refuses to start.
#
# Confirmations and tickets are queued with the confirming transaction,
# encrypted at rest, and retried independently of gateway callbacks.
#
# The compose stack points at mailpit, which catches every message and shows it at
# http://localhost:8026 — nothing is delivered to a real address in development.
SMTP_HOST=localhost
SMTP_PORT=1026
# starttls (default, correct for port 587) | tls (implicit, port 465) | none.
# "none" sends credentials and registration details in the clear: mailpit only.
SMTP_TLS=none
SMTP_USERNAME=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
# Microsoft Exchange Online authenticates with XOAUTH2 rather than a password:
# set all three of these and leave SMTP_PASSWORD empty. All three or none, and
# SMTP_USERNAME becomes required — XOAUTH2 authenticates as a named mailbox.
# The Entra app registration needs SMTP.SendAsApp with admin consent, Send As on
# the mailbox, and SMTP AUTH enabled for it; see docs/email.md.
# SMTP_OAUTH_TENANT_ID=
# SMTP_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# SMTP_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# The sender. A relay will usually reject a From on a domain it is not responsible
# for, so this has to be an address it accepts.
EMAIL_FROM=events@example.com

# An alternative to all of the above: send through the Microsoft Graph API
# instead of SMTP. Wins over SMTP when set — SMTP_HOST does not need to be
# configured at all in that case, EMAIL_FROM above is still the mailbox sent
# as. All three or none. This is a DIFFERENT app permission than SMTP OAuth
# above — Mail.Send (application, admin-consented), not SMTP.SendAsApp — the
# two are not interchangeable. See docs/email.md.
# GRAPH_TENANT_ID=
# GRAPH_CLIENT_ID=
# GRAPH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Optional, when replies should go somewhere other than EMAIL_FROM.
# EMAIL_REPLY_TO=
#
# Optional. Where a copy of each confirmed registration goes — the organiser. It
# also carries the oversold warning, which otherwise only exists in the logs.
# Leave it empty and the registrant's confirmation is the only mail sent.
NOTIFY_EMAIL=organiser@example.com

# --- Event images ---
# An event image is always bytes this server holds. There is no way to point an
# event at a URL on somebody else's server: those bytes can change or vanish, and
# an event page with a broken picture is worse than one with none.
#
# REQUIRED: one of the two backends below. They are mutually exclusive, so setting
# both is a boot failure — which one wins would otherwise be a guess — and setting
# neither is a boot failure too. The bar is low on purpose: IMAGE_DIR is one path
# and needs nothing running.
#
# The simplest option: a directory this server writes to and serves at /images/.
# One binary, one volume, no object storage to run. NOT for a deployment behind a
# load balancer or one that scales to zero — two instances do not share a directory,
# and a platform whose filesystem does not survive an instance loses them entirely.
IMAGE_DIR=.local/images
#
# Or object storage, which is what anything scaled out wants. All-or-nothing:
# BLOB_ENDPOINT with any of the rest missing is a boot failure, because the
# alternative is finding out at the first upload.
#
# Works against anything speaking the S3 API. For Google Cloud Storage, through
# its interoperability XML API with an HMAC key pair:
#   BLOB_ENDPOINT=storage.googleapis.com
# For Cloudflare R2:
#   BLOB_ENDPOINT=<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
#   BLOB_REGION=auto
# Production defaults live in deploy/: R2, with IMAGE_DIR unset.
# BLOB_ENDPOINT=<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
# BLOB_BUCKET=goevent-images
# BLOB_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# BLOB_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# BLOB_REGION=auto
# BLOB_USE_TLS=true
#
# Where images are *read* from, which is not where they are written to. The server
# writes through BLOB_ENDPOINT; a browser fetches through this. For R2 it is your
# custom domain or the pub-*.r2.dev address.
#
# The bucket has to be publicly readable at this address — images are served
# straight from it and never proxied through the server, which is what lets a CDN
# do the work.
# BLOB_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://images.example.com

# --- Rate limits ---
# Per client IP, per minute, on the three surfaces worth protecting. 0 turns one
# off — spelled out, because switching a protection off should be deliberate.
#
# The burst is a third of the allowance (minimum 2), so a person who double-clicks
# never trips one. RATE_LIMIT_LOGIN_PER_MINUTE=10 therefore means three attempts
# straight away, then one every six seconds.
#
# A throttled request gets 429 with Retry-After. That matters most on the payment
# callback: the gateway reads it and retries, which is why a throttled notification
# is not a lost payment — but it is why the callback's limit is the loosest.
# RATE_LIMIT_LOGIN_PER_MINUTE=10
# RATE_LIMIT_CHECKOUT_PER_MINUTE=20
# RATE_LIMIT_CALLBACK_PER_MINUTE=120
# RATE_LIMIT_STATUS_PER_MINUTE=30

# --- Proxies ---
# Where the address a request came from is read from. This describes what is
# actually in front of the server, and naming a header nothing is setting lets a
# client claim any source IP it likes — which is one of the checks on the payment
# callback, and the key the rate limits are counted against.
#
#   remote      (default) the connection's own address. Nothing in front.
#   forwarded   the leftmost X-Forwarded-For entry. Only behind a proxy that
#               *replaces* that header rather than appending to it — Caddy does;
#               nginx's $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for does not, and neither does
#               Cloudflare, tunnel or not. Behind Cloudflare, use "cloudflare".
#   cloudflare  CF-Connecting-IP. Only where Cloudflare is the *only* way in: a
#               tunnel, or an origin firewalled to Cloudflare's ranges. Anything
#               that can reach the server directly can forge that header.
#
# Behind Cloudflare, "forwarded" is the wrong answer and not a safe one:
# Cloudflare appends to X-Forwarded-For, so its leftmost entry is whatever the
# client sent.
# CLIENT_IP_SOURCE=remote

# --- Embedding ---
# Optional. Comma-separated origins allowed to frame these pages, for dropping
# an event page into a site hosted elsewhere. Empty means no framing at all.
# Use scheme://host[:port] with no trailing slash.
# EMBED_ORIGINS=https://www.example.com

# --- Fonts ---
# Optional. The default theme uses the system font stack, which needs no origin
# allowed and loads instantly, so both of these are empty by default.
#
# To use a hosted font service, set both: FONT_ORIGINS widens the CSP, and
# FONT_CSS_URL is the stylesheet the layout links. Setting only the first allows
# a font nothing asks for; setting only the second is refused at boot, because the
# CSP would block the stylesheet and the page would render in the fallback font
# with only a console warning to say why.
#
# For an Adobe Fonts (Typekit) kit, use the *CSS* embed from the Web Project
# panel, not the JavaScript one — the loader snippet needs script-src widened and
# a nonce for the inline <script> and <style> it injects, which this project does
# not support. Two origins are needed because the kit's stylesheet and the .woff2
# files it points at are served from different hosts:
# FONT_ORIGINS=https://use.typekit.net,https://p.typekit.net
# FONT_CSS_URL=https://use.typekit.net/abc1def.css
#
# Google Fonts is the same shape, with the CSS on one host and the files on
# another:
# FONT_ORIGINS=https://fonts.googleapis.com,https://fonts.gstatic.com
# FONT_CSS_URL=https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter&display=swap
#
# Either way the site's own stylesheet still has to *use* the family — set
# --font in a styles.css under STATIC_DIR. Widening the CSP and linking the kit
# makes the font available; it does not apply it.
#
# Whichever you pick, note that this puts a third-party request on every page
# including the registration form, and that a font CDN sees your visitors' IP addresses.
# Fonts under an open licence — most of Google's — can be self-hosted instead:
# drop the .woff2 into STATIC_DIR, reference it from your stylesheet with a
# /static/... URL, and leave both of these unset.

# --- Theming ---
# Optional. Templates in this directory override the embedded defaults by path:
# the directory is shaped like internal/handler/templates/, so pages/not_found.gohtml
# here replaces the definitions in pages/not_found.gohtml there. A file in the wrong
# subdirectory is simply never looked for.
# TEMPLATE_DIR=./templates
#
# Optional. Assets in this directory override the bundled ones by name — drop a
# logo.svg in here to rebrand without a rebuild — and new names are served too, for
# an overridden template that references its own hero.png. Only image, CSS, JS and
# font extensions are served, so a note left in here is not published.
# STATIC_DIR=./static
#
# Optional, development only. Re-read both directories on every request instead of
# once at startup, so editing a template or a stylesheet needs a page refresh
# rather than a restart. The compose stack sets it; a deployment must not, because
# it reparses every template per request and turns a broken override into a 500 at
# request time instead of a boot failure.
# THEME_RELOAD=false
